WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.767
Welcome to Module Five: Evaluation.

2
00:00:03.097 --> 00:00:05.629
It's about measuring what your model can actually do.

3
00:00:06.429 --> 00:00:10.155
Picture a team that fine-tunes a small model for customer support.

4
00:00:10.485 --> 00:00:14.552
Validation loss falls, the demo looks great, and they ship on Friday.

5
00:00:15.352 --> 00:00:17.371
By Monday, the tickets are piling up.

6
00:00:17.701 --> 00:00:21.534
The answers sound confident, but they quote the wrong refund policy.

7
00:00:22.334 --> 00:00:25.932
Worse, a benchmark score jumped, and nobody can explain why.

8
00:00:26.262 --> 00:00:29.113
Some test questions had leaked into the training data.

9
00:00:29.913 --> 00:00:31.762
The fix isn't one magic number.

10
00:00:32.092 --> 00:00:34.666
It's a set of measurements, each matched to a question.

11
00:00:35.466 --> 00:00:37.336
Lesson one starts with the metrics.

12
00:00:37.666 --> 00:00:39.926
Loss curves show whether training worked,

13
00:00:39.976 --> 00:00:42.800
and perplexity turns loss into an intuitive number.

14
00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:47.006
BLEU and ROUGE count words that overlap with a reference answer.

15
00:00:47.336 --> 00:00:51.638
But overlap can't see a wrong number, so a fluent, false answer still scores well.

16
00:00:52.438 --> 00:00:55.214
When answers are checkable, use exact match,

17
00:00:55.264 --> 00:00:58.489
or pass@k, which runs generated code against tests.

18
00:00:58.819 --> 00:01:02.695
Multiple-choice benchmarks score each option by its log-likelihood.

19
00:01:03.495 --> 00:01:07.413
Lesson two is a hands-on lab with lm-evaluation-harness.

20
00:01:07.743 --> 00:01:11.917
You'll benchmark a model before and after fine-tuning,

21
00:01:11.967 --> 00:01:14.263
on MMLU, HellaSwag, and GSM8K.

22
00:01:15.063 --> 00:01:17.817
You'll see which knobs silently change the numbers,

23
00:01:17.867 --> 00:01:19.685
like shot count and chat template.

24
00:01:20.015 --> 00:01:22.803
Between runs, change exactly one thing: the model.

25
00:01:23.603 --> 00:01:28.310
And you'll catch contamination by checking n-gram overlap with benchmark questions.

26
00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:32.835
At scale, NeMo Evaluator runs these checks against any served model.

27
00:01:33.635 --> 00:01:37.511
Lesson three tackles open-ended answers with no single reference.

28
00:01:37.841 --> 00:01:40.392
An LLM judge scores them with a rubric,

29
00:01:40.442 --> 00:01:43.593
and swapping answer order cancels position bias.

30
00:01:44.393 --> 00:01:47.757
A judge is a model too, so you check it against human labels.

31
00:01:48.087 --> 00:01:50.831
Cohen's kappa measures agreement beyond pure chance.

32
00:01:51.631 --> 00:01:55.464
For RAG systems, you evaluate retrieval and generation separately.

33
00:01:55.794 --> 00:01:59.733
Faithfulness asks whether every claim is backed by the retrieved context.

34
00:02:00.533 --> 00:02:03.197
Error analysis turns scores into fixes,

35
00:02:03.247 --> 00:02:06.606
and a domain eval set runs in CI on every change.

36
00:02:06.936 --> 00:02:09.233
That would have caught the refund bug before Friday.

37
00:02:10.033 --> 00:02:13.865
By the end, you'll benchmark, judge, and gate your own fine-tuned model.

38
00:02:14.195 --> 00:02:19.244
Evaluation is seven percent of the exam, so let's start with Lesson one: metrics.
